response_en:
To find the base-2 expression for $222_{10}$, we can repeatedly divide by 2 and keep track of the remainders.
\[
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\multicolumn{1}{r}{} & \multicolumn{1}{r}{} & \multicolumn{1}{r}{1} \\
\cline{2-3}
222 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{0} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
111 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{1} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
55 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{1} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
27 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{1} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
13 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{1} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
6 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{0} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
3 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{1} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
1 & \multicolumn{1}{|r}{1} & \downarrow \\
\cline{2-3}
\end{array}
\]
Reading the remainders from bottom to top, we get $222_{10}=11011110_2$.
The sum of the digits in the base-2 expression is $1+1+0+1+1+1+1+0=\boxed{6}$.
The answer is: 6

The product of three fractions is the product of their numerators divided by the product of their denominators.
So, the product of 1/2, 2/3, and 3/4 is $\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{3}{4} = \frac{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3}{2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4} = \frac{6}{24} = \boxed{\frac{1}{4}}$.
The answer is: \frac{1}{4}

